best practice on using typescript with rest api json objects
To handle REST API JSON objects in TypeScript optimally, you must achieve runtime data validation while maintaining 
strict static type definitions. Because TypeScript types disappear at compile time, treating an unknown API 
response as a trusted type without validating it can lead to uncaught runtime crashes if the backend payload 
changes unexpectedly.

1. Validate at the Network Edge (Avoid as Type)
Using type assertions like response.json() as User overrides TypeScript's type checker, tricking it into trusting 
unvalidated external data. Instead, use a runtime validation library like Zod.

Define a runtime schema: Let the library handle structural verification.Infer static types: Automatically generate types directly from that schema to avoid writing code twice.
import { z } from 'zod';

// 1. Define the structural runtime schema
export const UserSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string().uuid(),
  name: z.string(),
  email: z.string().email(),
  role: z.enum(['admin', 'user']),
  createdAt: z.string().transform((str) => new Date(str)), // Automatically formats JSON strings
});

// 2. Infer the TypeScript type from the Zod schema
export type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;



