2. Isolate Fetch Logic into Type-Safe Wrappers
Do not sprinkle raw fetch calls across UI components. Centralize them in dedicated, reusable API service modules that enforce incoming and outgoing data structures.
export async function fetchUser(userId: string): Promise<User> {
  const response = await fetch(`https://example.com{userId}`);
  
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);
  }
  
  const rawData = await response.json();
  
  // 3. Parse and validate the response against the schema at runtime
  return UserSchema.parse(rawData); 
}


3. Handle unknown Over any for Raw Payloads
When building generic utility functions, interceptors, or handling unexpected errors, never type incoming JSON payloads 
as any. Use unknown to force your code to manually perform a check before accessing nested fields.

// Good practice for a generic network error parser
export function parseApiError(errorPayload: unknown): string {
  if (
    errorPayload && 
    typeof errorPayload === 'object' && 
    'message' in errorPayload && 
    typeof (errorPayload as any).message === 'string'
  ) {
    return (errorPayload as { message: string }).message;
  }
  return 'An unexpected network error occurred.';
}

4. Separate Request Body Types from Response Types
Do not reuse a single data interface for every lifecycle stage of an object. An object coming from a GET request usually contains 
metadata fields (like server-side timestamps and auto-incrementing IDs) that a client-side POST request payload should not 
submit. Use utility types like Omit or Pick to quickly generate variations.

// The complete profile object coming from GET /users/1
export interface UserProfile {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  avatarUrl: string;
  createdAt: string;
}

// The exact data structure needed for PUT /users/1 (Omits read-only server fields)
export type UpdateUserProfileDto = Omit<UserProfile, 'id' | 'createdAt'>;

5. Standardize Wrapper Responses
API payloads usually come enveloped inside standard top-level JSON fields (like data, error, or pagination arrays). Use TypeScript 
generics (<T>) to type these envelopes safely across different endpoints.
export interface ApiResponseEnvelope<T> {
  data: T;
  meta: {
    timestamp: string;
    requestId: string;
  };
}

export interface PaginatedEnvelope<T> {
  items: T[];
  totalCount: number;
  currentPage: number;
}


6. Automate Definitions with OpenAPI / Swagger
If your backend team already maintains documentation using OpenAPI, do not manually copy-paste JSON formats into 
your frontend types. Use command-line utilities to generate everything straight from the spec file.
